{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent low bias: model forecasts are systematically below realized prices, especially from ~day 10 onward. Apply an upward level shift that increases with horizon (small in days 1–5, larger in days 15–30).",
    "Trend/acceleration underreaction: when the market transitions into an upswing (as in these samples), the model lags and misses the slope. Add a momentum term based on the last 5–10 days’ return; scale it up for horizons >10 days.",
    "Late-horizon catch-up failure: errors widen materially in the back half of the 30d window (often +2 to +6 €/t underprediction). Use a horizon-weighted correction (e.g., linear ramp) rather than a flat offset.",
    "Spike/rally underestimation: during sharp rallies the model predicts partial mean reversion too early and caps highs. If recent volatility/positive returns exceed a threshold, reduce mean-reversion strength and allow higher terminal levels.",
    "Shape mismatch (too flat): forecast paths are smoother than outcomes; realized series shows larger swings and higher peaks. Increase forecast variance conditional on recent realized volatility (volatility scaling)."
  ]
}
